The limit can also be found directly, without memorizing the three cases, as done here.

This calculation uses the fact that for any nonzero number $k$ and positive power $n$, the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}$ is equal to $0$.

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{3x^3-5x}{x^3-2x^2+1}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{3x^3-5x}{x^3}}{\dfrac{x^3-2x^2+1}{x^3}}\qquad\gray{\text{Divide both sides by }x^3}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{3\cancel{x^3}}{\cancel{x^3}}-\dfrac{5\cancel x}{\cancel x\cdot x^2}}{\dfrac{\cancel{x^3}}{\cancel{x^3}}-\dfrac{2\cancel{x^2}}{x\cdot\cancel{x^2}}+\dfrac{1}{x^3}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{3-\dfrac{5}{x^2}}{1-\dfrac{2}{x}+\dfrac{1}{x^3}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{3-0}{1-0+0}\qquad\gray{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}=0}
\\\\
&=3
\end{align}$
